From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 10:34:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] soc: apple: Add CPU cluster memory-bandwidth votes

With the PMP running, a streaming CPU gets the lowest fabric and DRAM
controller levels unless its cluster asks for more: on an M1 Max, CPU
STREAM bandwidth with the PMP running is half of what it is without it
(55/67 against 110/133 GB/s copy/triad, 10 threads; 25/29 against 52/71
with one thread). A request from each cluster's slot gives 160/165 and,
per cluster, 76/82 GB/s.

Holding the request all the time costs nothing measurable at idle, but
about 2.4 W under compute-bound load (sha256 on 10 threads, same
throughput), so it has to follow memory demand rather than CPU load.
Every 10 ms, for each CPU that was busy for at least half the period at
no less than 60% of its maximum frequency, read its L1D load misses and
active cycles from the CPU PMU. A cluster with a CPU at or above 10
misses per 1000 cycles (STREAM: 56-205; sha256: about 1) requests
200 GB/s from the PMP interconnect provider, and drops the request 100 ms
after its last such sample. Idle CPUs are not touched, and nothing is
sampled while the PMP is not running.

Measured on an M1 Max: STREAM 160-164 GB/s for 10 threads and 76/82
GB/s for one thread on either performance cluster; idle and sha256
power equal to no requests (25.6 against 25.9 W).

"mode" (auto, off, force) switches the requests off or holds them, for
A/B measurements; the thresholds are module parameters, and the
device's "stats" attribute shows each cluster's state and time with a
request. The interconnect provider registers the device once it has the
clusters' nodes (t6000/t6001); the driver is a module, loaded by its
platform alias.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
